  I'm not going to design a notification subsystem on top of four unbuilt ones and pretend the result is grounded. But I
  also don't want to turn your request into "let's build the whole app first" on my own authority. So the first fork is
  about what we're actually scoping.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether notifications is a feature of an app you're about to build here, or a component you're designing
  against a real app that lives somewhere else.

  - [1mGreenfield, notifications-first[0m — we design tasks/users/events only as thin as notifications requires, and build it
    here. Buys a working demo fastest; costs you a task model shaped by notification needs rather than product needs,
    which is awkward to unwind once real tasks arrive.
  - [1mDecompose, tasks first[0m — we spec tasks + identity as sub-project one, notifications as sub-project two. Buys a task
    model that's right on its own terms, and notifications gets real events to hang off. Costs a delay before anything
    notification-shaped exists. Each sub-project gets its own spec → plan cycle.
  - [1mReal app lives elsewhere[0m — this repo is a placeholder and the actual tasks app is another codebase. Then the whole
    design keys off that system's event model and I need to see it before designing anything. This is the one case where
    the other two answers would be wasted work.

  [1mMy recommendation: decompose, tasks first[0m — unless the real app lives elsewhere, in which case that dominates. The
  notification subsystem's hardest questions (what counts as a change, what "care about" means, how events are produced)
  are all answered by the task model. Designing them backwards means guessing, and guesses about a data model are the
  expensive kind to get wrong.
